    "text": {
     "en": "Agriculture is the backbone of Indian economy. The main seasons are Kharif (June to October), Rabi (October to March) and Zaid. Rice is a Kharif crop and wheat is a Rabi crop.\nMajor crops are rice, wheat, millets, pulses, sugarcane, cotton, jute, tea and coffee. Rice grows in the wet plains and deltas, wheat in the Ganga plains, tea in Assam and the Nilgiris and cotton in black soil regions.\nSoils include alluvial, black, red, laterite and desert soils. Irrigation by canals, tanks and wells supports farming. The Green Revolution brought high-yielding seeds, and issues like soil erosion need soil conservation."
    }
